  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe will use a multi-resolution optimization strategy with three main phases:\n\n- **Phase I: Global exploration** - For 200-300 seconds:\n  * Use Faraday optimization on coarse grids (48-96 points) to escape local minima\n  * Maintain population diversity through score-based sampling\n  * Use cyclic shifts and Fourier transforms to generate variants\n  * Apply gradient projection to low-cost candidates to prevent essential computational overhead\n\n- **Phase II: Local refinement** - For 300-600 seconds:\n  * For promising candidates, apply aggressive upsampling (to 1120 points)\n  * Use projected gradient methods with analytical gradients\n  * Implement multi-stage refinement with decreasing step sizes\n  * Employ trust-region optimization with adaptive radius\n\n- **Phase III: Ultra-fine polishing** - If time remains:\n  * Activate geometric programming relaxation on chunked representations\n  * Use continuous relaxation methods with controlled rounding\n  * Implement parallel local search on distinctly shifted populations\n  * Maintain record of intermediate solutions throughout\n\nKey strengths of this approach:\n- Combines global exploration with efficient local refinement\n- Upsampling adapts resolution dynamically around highly promising candidates\n- Gradient-based methods avoid costly finite-difference computations\n- Progressive resolution reduces sensitivity to initial conditions\n- Ensures valid solutions throughout with built-in repair operations\n\nPotential limitations:\n- Heavy computation at high resolutions may require parallelization\n- Geometric programming has explicit boundary handling\n- Ultra-fine polishing may require careful rounding strategies\n- Trade-off between search breadth and refinement depth\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n with \u2211h_i = n/2\n- n = 560 (base), 1120 (upsampled)\n- Integral constraint: \u2211h_i = n/2\n- Objective: minimize c = (2.0/n) * max_{lag} cross_correlation(h,1-h)\n\nDomain:\n- Base domain: [0,2] discretized into 560 samples\n- Cross-correlation: defined via np.correlate(mode=\"full\")\n\nRepair:\n- h = clip(h_lower, 0, 1)\n- Adjust sum to n/2 through uniform redistribution\n\nConstrained optimization:\n- Integral constraint handled via penalty function\n- Bounds enforced via projection\n- Lag selection: absolute maximum over all lags\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nValidate parent: ensure sum(initial_h_values)\u2248280 and all in [0,1]\n\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n    import time, numpy as np\n    from scipy.optimize import linear_constraint\n\n    t0 = time.time()\n    n0 = len(initial_h_values)  # 560\n    best_c = np.inf\n    best_h = initial_h_values.copy()\n\n    # Phase I: Global optimization with coarser grids (48-96 points)\n    time_budget = budget_s * 0.4\n    elapsed = time.time() - t0\n\n    # Faraday grid optimization (coarse grid)\n    # ...\n    # End of phase I code\n\n    # Phase II: Local refinement with upsampling\n    base_factor = 2.0\n    n1 = n0 * base_factor  # 1120\n    upsampled = np.repeat(best_h, 2)[:n1]\n\n    # Local projection and repair\n    # ...\n    # End of phase II code\n\n    # Phase II refinement loop\n    # ...\n    # End of phase II refinement\n\n    # Phase III: If time remains\n    if time_remaining(elapsed, budget_s) > 300:\n        # ...\n        # Parallelization implemented here\n\n    return (best_h_curr, best_c_curr, best_h_curr.size)\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Coarse grid sizes: [n0, n0*0.5, n0*0.75], upsample only top 3%\n- uses_initializer: \n  - \"fourier\": transform to frequency domain & apply perturbations\n  - \"cyclic\": random cyclic permutations of winner\n  - \"projection\": orthogonal projection onto constraint space\n- Reward function: combine finish time with improvement rate\n- trusted_magnitude: fractional (0.8-0.95) but adaptive per round\n- Rounding strategy: controlled rounding by magnitude class\n\n5. Computational budget\n- Coarse grid (48-96 points): ~8-16 evaluations/second\n- Base resolution: ~5-10 seconds\n- Upsampled resolution: ~15-20 function-evaluations/second\n- Global phase: 15-25% of budget\n- Local phase: 50-70% of budget\n- Polishing: 10-20% of budget if time remains\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Validate final solution:\n  - all(0 <= x <= 1 for x in final_h)\n  - abs(sum(final_h) - n/2) < 1e-5\n  - Use identical correlation function for validation\n\n- Score comparison:\n  - Keep candidate if score <= current best with strict tolerance (1e-10)\n\n- Strict record keeping:\n  - Store history of successful evaluations\n  - Track local optima frequency per round\n\n- Success criteria:\n  - 0.3795 < final_c < 0.3808\n\n- Safety mechanisms:\n  - Progressive repair mechanisms\n  - Fallback to lower-resolution representation if invalid\n  - Budget-aware slicing for parallel capabilities",
